; Regression: a dotted call PREPENDS ITS RECEIVER, and the arity was not checked
; against that (2026-08-15).
;
; A call on a value receiver passes the receiver as argument 0, so the receiver
; always occupies one parameter — but WHICH one depends on the member. A `this`
; method binds it to its declared `this`. A this-LESS member has the receiver
; fill its FIRST DECLARED PARAMETER: `old.is_equal(gid)` against
; `is_equal(a: pointer[ELObj], b: pointer[ELObj])` passes old as `a` and gid as
; `b`, and packages/dazzle depends on exactly that.
;
; So `b.one(21)` against a this-less `one(c: int)` is one argument too many: the
; receiver already fills `c`. It planned with rc 0 and emitted
;   call @one(ptr <receiver>, i64 21)
; into a ONE-parameter function — the receiver landed in `c`, the real argument
; was dropped, and emit_call builds the callee's type from the ARGUMENTS, so
; llc saw nothing wrong either. The callee simply answered its own receiver's
; address.
;
; Found while porting the TypeScript scanner: `this.is_identifier_start(cp)` on
; a this-less predicate answered the receiver's address for every code point, so
; the identifier scan never advanced and the loop spun until the process was
; killed. The infinite loop was the lucky part — the same wrong answer in a
; predicate that drives no loop is just a wrong result.
;
; ★ The check CANNOT live in lookup_method#, where the neighbouring arity gate
; is. That function also serves a this-less member called UNQUALIFIED from a
; sibling (`allocate_page()` inside Page.allocate_exclusive_page), where nothing
; is prepended and args = params exactly — so its gate has to accept both
; readings, and that disjunction is what let this through. Tightening it there
; was tried and refuted by the build: the stdlib's own Page.allocate_page and
; Page.release_page sibling calls fail it. Whether a receiver exists is known
; only at the call site, so the gate lives there (report_receiver_arity#).
;
; xfail: wrong number of arguments for one: got 1, expected 0

define Box
(
    v: int
)
{
    function one(c: int) returns int
    {
        c * 2
    }
}

let b Box(7)
let r b.one(21)
print "unreachable"
